! jb_ax5b "I-Box 5", routine 1260's DS block -- the deck's own test
! that a STORE INTO THE PERMANENTLY PROTECTED AREA sets DS when the
! interruption system is enabled.  PDF pp.43-44 (printed pp.40-41),
! octal 021365.00-021376.00, transcribed VERBATIM at the listing's own
! addresses and checked bit-exactly by verify_ax5b_16086_listing.py.
!
! WHY THIS BLOCK.  Storage protection and the interruption mechanism
! were both implemented from the Reference Manual (p.31, p.32, p.45,
! p.48) and tested only by hand-written programs -- the WEAKER of this
! project's two standards, and said so in their own headers.  This is
! the deck that tested the same hardware on the real machine, and its
! own error message states the rule this model was built to:
!
!     "SV TO 1.0 FAILS TO SET DS WHEN ENABLED."
!
! Three facts in eight words, each of which storage_protection.strap
! asserts from the manual alone:
!   * the store's destination is WORD 1 -- one of p.31's permanently
!     protected locations 1, 2 and 3, and not the word 3 this project
!     happened to pick;
!   * the indicator is DS;
!   * the gating is "WHEN ENABLED", which is the p.45 clause that put
!     the interrupt-enable bit into this model in the first place.
!
! AND IT POLLS DS WITH THE SYSTEM OFF.  The BDS that reads the
! indicator comes after "16086A BD,$+.32", not before it.  That is the
! trap this project paid for and wrote into docs/NEXT.md -- "a program
! cannot poll DS while enabled", because DS is permanently masked and
! the mechanism turns it off before the next instruction runs.  The
! deck's author knew; the disable is right there in the listing.
!
! WHAT THE PAGE ITSELF SAYS, as against what is read into it.  The
! deck's own comments on these nineteen rows are only five: "-DS",
! "-ILLEGAL OP, FP 31.", "-TURN ON $AD ON", and the two-line error
! "SV TO 1.0 FAILS TO SET / DS WHEN ENABLED."  Everything below that
! is not one of those five is marked.
!
! GROUNDED IN THE PAGE OR THE MANUAL:
!   SV,$X4,1.00        a store into word 1, one of p.31's permanently
!                      protected locations.  The block is headed "-DS"
!                      and its error names this instruction as "SV TO
!                      1.0", so this is the provocation.
!   SV,$X4,1.32        the deck says "TURN ON $AD ON".  16083, the AD
!                      block on p.41, opens with the same instruction
!                      under the comment "-AD".
!   LX / SX            LX and SX move a WHOLE 64-bit index word
!                      (StretchExecute.bsv's dixop_LX/dixop_SX use
!                      unpackIndexWord/packIndexWord, unlike LV/SV
!                      which touch only the value half).  So
!                      "LX,$X4,16086 / SX,$X4,16085B+15.0" copies the
!                      whole word at 021365 -- this block's own
!                      SV,$X4,1.00 half-word together with the 3740
!                      datum beside it -- into 16085B word 15, and
!                      160K16's word into 16085B word 16.
!   16085B word N      p.48: the interruption table is "48 successive
!                      full-word locations" and the developed
!                      indicator number is added to the interruption
!                      address, so word N is indicator N -- 15 is OP
!                      and 16 is AD.  That units rule is what
!                      interrupt_pg0.strap pins on this model.
!   LV,$X4,$IA / SVA,$X5,$IA / SVA,$X4,$IA
!                      save the interruption address register, point
!                      it at 16085B, restore it afterwards.  Word 2 is
!                      $IA (Ref. Manual p.48 and opcodes.py).
!   BE,16086A / 16086A BD,$+.32
!                      enable, then disable.  p.40: both act
!                      unconditionally and before the branch.
!   BDS,16086B         branch on DS.  It is AFTER the BD, never
!                      before -- the deck reads the indicator with the
!                      system off.
!   BADZ,$+.32         branch on AD and zero it (the trailing-Z
!                      grammar, C22-6531 Appendix A footnote 2), then
!                      B,16088.
!
! NOT TRACED, and left as questions rather than guesses:
!   * WHY the block plants its own protected store into interrupt
!     table entry 15 (OP) and 160K16 into entry 16 (AD).  The DD
!     commented "ILLEGAL OP, FP 31." sits in the same word as that
!     store, so the copied entry is {store, illegal op} -- which
!     reads like OP's routine being made to provoke something in
!     turn, but nothing on these two pages says so.  16082 (OP) and
!     16083 (AD) on pp.41-42 are where that would be settled.
!   * WHAT 160K16 contains.  Only its address, 023061.00, is printed
!     here.
!   * WHETHER the DD is ever executed on the taken path, or is only
!     data for the copy above.  Reaching it would require falling
!     through the SV, which the listing's own flow does.
!
! WHAT THIS IS AND IS NOT.  It is an ENCODING oracle, like
! ibox1_ensu.strap: IBM's own assembler printing nineteen half-words,
! checked field for field against what this project's assembler
! produces.  It is NOT run on the model -- the block is a fragment of
! routine 1260 and needs 16085B's NOP table (built by 16085A), the
! SEN/SERS error harness and 160K16 before it could execute.
! storage_protection.strap remains the behavioural test and remains
! hand-written; what changes is that its rule now has a deck behind it.
!
! SUBSTITUTIONS: three, all of them notation, none of them semantic.
!   1. This deck labels its blocks with NUMBERS (16086, 16086A,
!      16086B, 16085B, 16093, 16088) and this assembler reads a
!      leading digit as an address, so every such label is prefixed
!      with "L".
!   2. The listing's "%8@...@" radix brackets are written in this
!      project's "(8)" form.
!   3. The listing's "%BU,32,8@" data description is written
!      "(BU,32,8)".
! Nothing else is changed: operands, order, addresses and the DD's
! value are the page's own text.
!
! A BYPRODUCT WORTH RECORDING.  "B,16088" prints as "21400.50 00",
! and bit 18 is the top bit of that octal pair (listing_oracle.py), so
! it is ONE and 16088 sits at 021400.40.  A 200 dpi render of p.45 had
! given 021400.00 for that row, which made the page look a half-word
! short; re-read at 600 dpi the column prints 021400.40, agreeing with
! the encoding.  Two witnesses, one printed and one encoded, and with
! them p.45 is self-consistent: the two TI instructions there are FULL
! WORDS at ODD half-words (021377.40 and 021400.40, legal per
! Reference Manual p.39), each straddling a word boundary, and the
! "BE,$+.32  -ENABLE AND AWAIT INTERRUPT" after them lands at
! 021401.40 with its own printed target 021402.00.
!
! NOTE, because the check cannot say it: L16088's 021400.40 is a SYN
! above, an INPUT to verify_ax5b_16086_listing.py.  That row passing
! is not evidence for the address; the two readings above are.  What
! the row adds is that this assembler places bit 18 where the listing
! places it.

! External symbols, given by SYN at the addresses this same listing
! prints for them.  Rows naming one check the opcode and modifier bits
! only; their address came from the same print.
L16085B SYN, (8)21261.0            ! the all-NOP interrupt table (p.43)
L160K16 SYN, (8)23061.0            ! a constant in the 160K block
SEN     SYN, (8)1310.0             ! the SIC error-entry link
SERS    SYN, (8)1304.0             ! the error-report routine
L16093  SYN, (8)21734.0            ! the "next test" exit
L16088  SYN, (8)21400.40           ! the per-indicator interrupt walk;
                                   !   .40, see the header

        SLC, (8)21365.0
        CNOP                       ! already word-aligned: emits nothing
L16086  SV,  $X4, 1.00             ! -DS          store into protected
                                   !              word 1
        DD(BU,32,8), (8)3740       ! -ILLEGAL OP, FP 31.
        SV,  $X4, 1.32             ! -TURN ON $AD ON
        LX,  $X4, L16086           !              this block's own first
                                   !              half-word, as data
        SX,  $X4, L16085B+15.0     !              into table word 15 (OP)
        LX,  $X4, L160K16
        SX,  $X4, L16085B+16.0     !              into table word 16 (AD)
        LV,  $X4, $IA              !              save the interruption
                                   !              address register
        LVI, $X5, L16085B
        SVA, $X5, $IA              !              point it at the NOP table
        BE,  L16086A               !              enable
L16086A BD,  $+.32                 !              disable, so DS can be read
        SVA, $X4, $IA              !              restore the saved address
        BDS, L16086B               !              DS set -> pass
        SIC, SEN
        B,   SERS                  ! -SV TO 1.0 FAILS TO SET
        B,   L16093                ! -DS WHEN ENABLED.
L16086B BADZ, $+.32                !              clear AD on the way out
        B,   L16088
        END, L16086
